Part Two

Forms and Service Mechanisms Article 8 (1) Forms of services to victims organized by PPT include:
a. medical services, in the form of treatment and recovery of physical injuries aimed at restoring the physical condition of victims carried out by medical personnel and paramedics;
b. medicolegal services, which are forms of medical services for evidentiary purposes in the legal field;
c. psychosocial services, which are services provided by companions in order to restore the traumatic condition of victims, including the provision of safe houses to protect victims from threats and intimidation for victims and provide social support so that victims have self-confidence, strength, and independence in resolving their problems;
d. legal services to assist victims in undergoing the judicial process;
e. economic independence services in the form of services for skills training and providing economic access so that victims can be independent.

(2) Service mechanisms as referred to in paragraph (1) are organized according to standard operating procedures (SOP) which will be regulated further by Regent Regulation.

2.5.text:
CHAPTER V"

SANCTION PROVISIONS

Article 12 (1) Any person who intentionally does not provide protection to women and children resulting in the occurrence of violence, allows violence to occur, and or does not report and does not provide protection to victims, shall be subject to sanctions in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations;

2. If officials appointed to implement protection do not carry out their obligations and responsibilities, they shall be subject to sanctions in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations;
3. PPT managers who carry out service tasks that violate service principles may be subject to sanctions in accordance with PPT internal mechanisms.

CHAPTER IV

OBLIGATIONS AND RESPONSIBILITIES

Article 6 (1) The Regional Government is obligated and responsible to carry out efforts to prevent the occurrence of violence, in the form of: a. collecting data and information about women and children victims of violence and laws and regulations;
b. conducting education on anti-violence values against women and children;
c. conducting socialization of laws and regulations related to the implementation of protection for women and children victims of violence;
d. conducting monitoring and evaluation of the implementation of protection for women and children victims of violence.
2. To anticipate the occurrence of acts of violence, the Regional Government is obligated to provide and organize services for victims in the form of:a. establishing and facilitating the implementation of integrated service institutions for victims by involving community elements;
b. encouraging public awareness of the importance of protection for victims.
3. The Regional Government in carrying out obligations as referred to in paragraph (1) and (2) must pay attention to the rights and obligations of parents, guardians, husbands or other persons who are legally responsible for the victim.

